THE REFUSAL LOG. Append-only evidence, MACHINE-WRITTEN by .claude/run_checked.sh, which is the only
writer. Do not edit entries by hand and do not delete them: an entry is the only surviving trace that
some instrument in this chain refused a real run, and the chain's own evidentiary standard is
capability shown on real output.

WHY IT EXISTS (user ruling, 2026-09-07). This chain archives its PASSES into git forever — every
commit message carries a gate's DONE line, written by commit_checked.sh and unamendable afterwards —
and forgot its REFUSALS entirely, because a refusing run reaches no commit. So the runs that actually
demonstrate a check can fire were exactly the ones being discarded. citation_crosscheck has refused on
the live corpus and that refusal survives only in a chat transcript, with its own output already gone.
It cannot be retro-filled; it is named here as the loss that motivated the file.

THE LOSS, STATED PLAINLY (user, 2026-09-07), because a file whose subject is discarded evidence should
not be vague about the piece of evidence it discarded: this log was built FOR a refusal that had already
happened, was demonstrated live to a SCRATCH file rather than to itself, and cannot store the instance
that motivated it. THE STORAGE ARRIVED ONE COMMIT AFTER THE EVIDENCE. That is not the same as the
by-design one-commit lag described below — that lag is between a refusal and its archiving, and it
recurs forever; this was a one-time gap between the instrument and the observation that argued for it,
and the observation lost.

HOW TO READ IT. `git log -p .claude/refusals.log` is the history of this chain refusing. Entries carry
a UTC timestamp, the reason (exit=N, or marker-absent for a vacuous run), the DONE marker that names
the instrument, the tool's filename, and the tail of the refusing run's own output with the full line
count so the excerpt never pretends to be complete. What an entry deliberately omits, and why, is in
run_checked.sh's header.

REFUSALS ARCHIVE ONE COMMIT LATE, by construction and not by accident. The run that appends here did
not commit — that is what refusing means — so the entry lands in the diff of the next SUCCESSFUL
commit. commit_checked.sh stages this file before committing, so that happens without anyone
remembering to.

THIS HEADER IS HAND-MAINTAINED; THE ENTRIES BELOW IT ARE NOT, and that is the only division in the
file. Everything above the first `==== REFUSAL` line is prose a person may correct or extend; nothing
from that line down is. Seeded by hand for the same reason .claude/record_count.json was: the
declaration in battery.py's NON_INSTRUMENTS has to exist before the file does, and a reader diffing an
unexplained artifact deserves to find its explanation inside it.

THE FIRST ENTRY ARRIVED 2026-09-07 AND BROKE THE FILE, which is worth more than a clean start. The seed
above ended WITHOUT a trailing newline, so deploy_check's refusal was appended onto the header's last
sentence — and `grep -c '^==== REFUSAL '`, the only thing that counts entries here and what
run_checked.sh's arms are built on, matched nothing. THE LOG HELD ONE REFUSAL AND REPORTED ZERO. A
zero-report over present data is the exact class this chain exists to refuse, produced by the chain's
own newest artifact on its first real use. Fixed at the WRITER (run_checked.sh's log_refusal now anchors
any append, and its arm 7 fails without that guard), not only here, because this header is
hand-maintained prose and the next edit could drop the terminator again. The entry below was re-seated
on its own line; its bytes were not touched.

AND IT IS A GENUINE REFUSAL, not a demonstration: deploy_check declined to certify a deploy because
HEAD was one commit ahead of origin/main, so nothing it measured could be a check on HEAD. That is the
fourth layer being right, and it is the kind of run this file was built to keep.

